  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a quantum error-correction workflow in which information from many physical qubits is used to infer whether an error has occurred in a logical qubit. On the left, a small lattice of circles represents a qubit layout containing two roles: “data qubit” and “ancilla qubit.” A boxed region marks the logical qubit encoded across several physical qubits rather than stored in a single one.\n\nThe middle of the figure shows a “soft readout,” suggesting that the measurements are not simply binary outcomes but richer analog signals from the qubit array. These measurement results are then passed to a decoding stage, where two approaches are compared: a conventional “Model” and an “AI” method. Both are depicted as taking the same measurement information as input.\n\nOn the right, the decoded result is a map of the qubit array with one qubit highlighted in red and labeled “Error detected,” indicating successful identification of a fault within the larger encoded system. Overall, the graphic emphasizes the pipeline from noisy quantum measurements to error detection, and highlights AI-based decoding as an alternative to traditional model-based methods.",
